DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The critical reception of Dussel's account of Eurocentrism spans several
strands: (1) ontological/metaphysical objections (Irvine) charging that
his "trans-ontology" of the Other is internally inconsistent; (2)
feminist critiques (Lange, Schutte) charging that liberation philosophy
romanticizes indigenous peoples and remains gender-blind; (3)
historiographical reconsiderations of the 1492 thesis and world-system
claims; (4) sympathetic internal critiques (Vizcaino, Stromback,
Maldonado-Torres, Sager and Spencer) that revise specific tenets; and
(5) external worries about decoloniality's pitfalls (e.g., as articulated
in debates referenced in this domain). Recent work (Timmenga et al. 2025,
Pinton 2025, Stromback 2024) revisits whether Dussel's ego conquiro and
alterity remain defensible.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain enables an honest assessment of Dussel's claims about
Eurocentrism by foregrounding their internal tensions, gendered blind
spots, and contested normative grounds. It also clarifies how sympathetic
critics propose to revise rather than abandon the project.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Pachon Soto's Spanish-language critiques and some external analytic
philosophy responses were not retrievable via CrossRef; further searches
in Spanish-language databases would help. Hull 2021 (pitfalls of
decoloniality) was not located in the cached files.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Ontological/metaphysical critique: Irvine
- Feminist critique: Lange, Schutte (cited via PhilPapers)
- Internal/sympathetic revision: Vizcaino, Stromback, Sager and Spencer, Maldonado-Torres
- Historiographical/contextual critique: Sharman, Vargas
- Defenses/elaborations: Timmenga et al., Alcoff, Colmenares Lizarraga
====================================================================
}

